## **Tight Wealth Indicator Options**\
\
Here are some tight, distinctive options in your influences' style:\
\
**Adams-style absurdist precision:**\
"Eloise sat uprightly in a chair that had cost more than most people's houses, though admittedly not as much as most people's funerals."\
\
**Pratchett-style understated observation:**\
"Eloise sat uprightly, her suit the kind that whispered 'expensive' so quietly that only accountants and undertakers could hear it."\
\
**Vonnegut-style matter-of-fact darkness:**\
"Eloise sat uprightly, wearing enough money to solve world hunger for a weekend."\
\
**Robbins-style whimsical specificity:**\
"Eloise sat uprightly, her business suit worth approximately seventeen yachts, or one decent politician."\
\
**Your voice synthesis:**\
"Eloise sat uprightly in a chair that cost less than her handbag, which cost less than her shoes, which together cost more than Sarah's life insurance payout."\
\
My preference is the Pratchett-style one - it's subtle, fits the scene's tone, and establishes wealth without disrupting the tension. The "whispered expensive" concept feels very much in your established voice.\
